  "result": "I stopped without a passing gate: both runs (2 of 6) returned **SCOPE_VIOLATION**, so nothing I wrote has been built or tested. The gate's scope check flags temp files the agent runtime writes under `.runtime/tmp/claude-154266/…`, not anything in the two node files. One of those files is the output of the gate command itself, so every run makes a new one and would fail the same way. Some come from other sessions running at the same time (LVMC_11_18_020, LVMC_11_30_050, LVMC_11_43_010_B_o1). The fix is on the harness side: leave `.runtime/tmp/` out of the scope check. It re-runs the gate after this session ends.\n\n**Rule** (`library/TrafficSpec/Rules/LVMC_11/LVMC_11_20_060_o2.lean`):\n- **Subject:** a vehicle, written as `ru_aggregate(subject, \"DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle\")`.\n- **When it applies:** the district assumption holds, the subject occupies a driveway/alley-entrance region or an alley region, and it also occupies a sidewalk region.\n- **Duty:** for every pedestrian other than the subject, two things must hold:\n  - It is not the case that the subject is moving faster than `p.halt_speed_tolerance` while the closest approach (within `p.conflict_horizon`) comes nearer than `p.conflict_centre_clearance`.\n  - The two never touch.\n- **Declarations:** unchanged from the skeleton. I added no narrowings because the node's text settled every choice.\n\n**Witnesses:** a roadway, a 4 m sidewalk, a driveway crossing it, a car with a 2 m half-length, and a pedestrian walking west at 1.5 m/s.\n\n| Witness | Scene | Expected |\n|---|---|---|\n| forbid | Car moving at 1 m/s; closest approach about 0.3 m within about 2.8 s; no contact | violated |\n| permit | Car stopped; closest approach 2.7 m, then the pedestrian walks away | compliant |\n| idle | Car on the roadway only | not applicable |\n| blind | The forbid scene with the car's speed hidden | unknown |\n| branch_1 | The forbid scene with no pedestrian | compliant |\n\n`task_result.md` in the working directory records both gate runs and the cause. I found nothing wrong with the node itself.",
